To see this, we start from the estimation bias of the largest and smallest eigenvalues of $\Sigma_0$. Let $\lambda_{\max}(X)$ and $\lambda_{\min}(X)$ be the largest eigenvalue and the smallest eigenvalue of the matrix $X$, respectively.
Since $\lambda_{\max}(\cdot)$ is a convex function and $\lambda_{\min}(\cdot)$ is a concave function over the space of symmetric matrices, then by Jensen's inequality, it holds that
\begin{align}
    \lambda_{\max}(\Sigma_0)=\lambda_{\max}(\mathbb{E}_n[\widehat\Sigma]) \leq \mathbb{E}_n[\lambda_{\max}(\widehat\Sigma)]~\text{and}~\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma_0)=\lambda_{\min}(\mathbb{E}_n[\widehat\Sigma]) \geq \mathbb{E}_n[\lambda_{\min}(\widehat\Sigma)],\label{ineq:Jensen}
\end{align}
where $\mathbb{E}_n$ denotes the expectation with respect to the $n$-product measures of the data-generating distribution $\mathbb{P}$ and the expectation is taken on $\widehat\Sigma$. Inequalities in~\eqref{ineq:Jensen} reveal that, on average, $\lambda_{\max}(\widehat\Sigma)$ overestimates its true counterpart $\lambda_{\max}(\Sigma_0)$, while $\lambda_{\min}(\widehat\Sigma)$ underestimates its true counterpart $\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma_0)$. 
The spectral bias occurs not only in the estimation of the largest and smallest eigenvalues but also in the estimation of all other eigenvalues.
Let $\lambda(\Sigma_0)\triangleq (\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_p)$ be the eigenvalues of $\Sigma_0$, and $\lambda(\widehat\Sigma)\triangleq (\widehat\lambda_1,\ldots,\widehat\lambda_p)$ be the eigenvalues of $\widehat\Sigma$, both in increasing order. In~\cite{ref:LEDOIT2004365}, the authors point out that, on average, $\lambda(\widehat\Sigma)$ is more dispersed than $\lambda(\Sigma_0)$, in the sense that
\[
    \frac{1}{p}\mathbb{E}_n\left[\sum_{i=1}^p (\widehat\lambda_i-\overline{\lambda})^2\right] = \frac{1}{p}\sum_{i=1}^p (\lambda_i-\overline{\lambda})^2 + \mathbb{E}_n[\|\widehat\Sigma-\Sigma_0\|_F^2] > \frac{1}{p}\sum_{i=1}^p (\lambda_i-\overline{\lambda})^2,
\]
where $\overline{\lambda}\triangleq \frac{1}{p}\sum_{i=1}^p \lambda_i$.
The inequality above implies that systematic bias may occur in the estimation of all eigenvalues in a similar manner of~\eqref{ineq:Jensen}: 
\begin{align}\label{bias-2}
    \begin{cases}
    \text{$\widehat\lambda_i$ overestimates $\lambda_i$,} & \text{when $\lambda_i$ is large,} \\
    \text{$\widehat\lambda_i$ underestimates $\lambda_i$,} & \text{when $\lambda_i$ is small.}
    \end{cases}
\end{align}

\begin{align}\label{bias-2}
    \begin{cases}
    \text{$\widehat\lambda_i$ overestimates $\lambda_i$,} & \text{when $\lambda_i$ is large,} \\
    \text{$\widehat\lambda_i$ underestimates $\lambda_i$,} & \text{when $\lambda_i$ is small.}
    \end{cases}
\end{align}

