\label{prop:saddle-point}
    If Assumptions~\ref{ass:regularity-nominal} and~\ref{ass:convex-divergence} hold, then the tuple $(\Sigma = \Sigma^\star, X = (\Sigma^\star)^{-1}, S=\Sigma^\star)$ is a saddle point of function $f(\Sigma, X, S)$, i.e., for any $(\Sigma, X) \in {\mathbb{S}^p_{++}} \times {\mathbb{S}^p_{++}}$,
    and $S\in \left\{S\in{\mathbb{S}^p_{+}}:D(S, \widehat\Sigma)\leq  \rho\right \}$,
    \begin{align}
        \label{eq:saddle}
        f\left(\Sigma^\star, (\Sigma^\star)^{-1}, S\right)
        \leq
        f\left(\Sigma^\star, (\Sigma^\star)^{-1},\Sigma^\star\right)
        \leq f\left(\Sigma,X,\Sigma^\star\right).
    \end{align}
    The saddle point is 
    an optimal solution to $\min_{\Sigma,X \in {\mathbb{S}^p_{++}}}~F(\Sigma,X)$.

For fixed $\Sigma=\Sigma^\star\in {\mathbb{S}^p_{++}}$, a basic calculation of the first-order optimality condition shows that 
$(\Sigma^\star,(\Sigma^\star)^{-1})$ 
is the minimizer of $\min_{\Sigma,X\in {\mathbb{S}^p_{++}}} f(\Sigma,X,\Sigma^\star)$.
Thus, the second inequality
of~\eqref{eq:saddle} holds.

